  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Monday)\nIssued at 1110 PM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nWarm weather will continue through the Mid-South through the\nforecast period. The feature responsible is an unseasonably\namplified high pressure system which has remained parked over the\nsoutheastern CONUS through the last week. Current forecast\nguidance remains bullish on the idea that this ridge will not\nonly remain over the Mid-South, but retrograde west and\nstrengthen through Thursday to around 600 dm. Surface\ntemperatures are likely to reach the upper 90s, if not rising at\nor above 100 F. Regardless, surface dewpoints will stay around\n70 F, leading to heat indices above 100 F. Most areas will see\n105+ F heat indices Tuesday, prompting an areawide Heat Advisory\nthrough Tuesday evening at 7 PM CDT. There is potential for the\nHeat Advisory to be extended into Wednesday, but dewpoints may\nmanage to mix down to a point where afternoon heat index values\nfail to reach the 105 F threshold for an advisory. Therefore, we\nhave opted to hold off on an extension, but there is a decent\nprobability that one will occur Tuesday.\n\nIn terms of precipitation, isolated, daily afternoon convection\nis still possible even in the midst of a strengthening and\nunseasonably strong ridge. RAP forecast soundings for both\nTuesday and Wednesday afternoons show a strengthening subsidence\ninversion around 500 mb, but surface theta e values are\nsufficiently high to produce surface based profiles supportive of\nisolated convection. Therefore, expect pop-up showers and\nthunderstorms both afternoons, especially across eastern Arkansas\nand the Missouri Bootheel and along an arc across northern\nMississippi. West Tennessee is less likely to see any\nprecipitation since the ridge is forecast to be centered over\nthis region.\n\nThrough the middle of the week, a cutoff low will form along the\neast coast and retrograde southwestward towards the Florida\nPeninsula. This trough will eventually continue through the Gulf\nalong the Gulf Coast into the latter half of the week. In\nresponse, the ridge is currently expected to flatten out to\naround 590 - 594 dm, which would correspond to a slight decrease\nin afternoon temperatures. However, the amplitude of the ridge,\neven after weakening, would still be above normal for this time\nof year. Therefore, above-normal temperatures in the mid 90s will\ncontinue into next weekend. Ensembles have converged onto a\nsolution which then brings 500 mb heights down below 590 dm by\nSunday, but which also includes 850 mb temps around 20 C,\nequivalent to 95 F through boundary layer mixing. So, even after\nthe ridge weakens substantially by this weekend, temperatures\nwill still continue to remain above normal. Isolated\nprecipitation associated with convection from the subtle height\nfalls could produce local heat relief. Forecast uncertainty\nballoons exponentially after Saturday, but CPC guidance still\nsuggests that above normal temperatures can be expected into next\nweek.",
